rota lap 4618, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; derived row 3 had been read once today and row 1 by nobody, so I advanced by hand to the unread row. Its test is tactile: press a boundary, and if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish. Applied to a gate's refusal, the hand went straight through -- the gate holds, and reaching for WHERE finds nothing.
think A cold roster pass was already in flight at my HEAD when I opened, so I held still and read rather than launching a second. It closed tree_moved=no, which is the still-tree reading my own thread's recommend asked for.
think Two reds: stash_record, and standing_equipment derivative behind it. Reds first, and the derivative one needed no separate thought.
think stash_record named its own route in the refusal -- run the scan with `list` -- which is REDS %592's repair working exactly as it was built to. Following it took one line.
think It named one unlanded record. path_absence_scan answered verdict=absent, here=no upstream=no, so the file was genuinely carried by nothing rather than merely missing from a stale checkout.
think Reading stash@{0} whole showed the record was the small part: THIRTEEN files, a complete BAKERY lap from 20260911.014546, stashed by its own round open at 015620 and never landed.
obs The lap's three guard files had no peer edit since the stash, so they were recovered with `git checkout stash@{0} -- <path>` -- the TREE rather than the blob, so 100755 rode along on both shell scripts (the exec-bit law's shorter road).
obs The seven SHARED files were re-applied by hand instead. A snapshot of a shared file is a rollback, which is %592's own sentence, and seven peers had written into REDS, ITINERARY and the two session rosters in the two hours since.
obs The row it booked as %702 had to be renumbered twice, which is the derived-spine law paying for itself in one lap. xy had published its own %702 while the lap sat stashed, so the spine's --next answered 704; then the send's own rebase brought down a peer's PUBLISHED %704, stamped 20260911.003518 against my row's 014147. The earlier stamp loses here and rule 3 says why: a published number never moves, and renumbering is confined to unshared rows. Mine was unshared, so it is %705.
obs REDS.md stood at exactly 40960 of 40960 -- zero bytes free, which the parked lap's own log had already named as the fleet's real choke. I folded %701, the one BOOKED row, to make room; the rebase then showed a peer had folded the SAME row onto its own shelf minutes earlier, so my shelf file was a second home for one row and the monotone gate would have refused it. Dropped, upstream's fold kept, and the row still fit: 1993 free against a row tightened from 2173 to 1977.
obs The shared ITINERARY page itself was left alone -- 19 bytes free, the most-written file on the pier, and a two-hour-old rewrite of it is the rollback this recovery exists to avoid. Its SHELF file was carried, which is a different thing: a standalone new archive page, and the live card's BAKERY block has since moved on to a later lap, so the account it holds really did leave the card.
obs That distinction was not mine to make by taste -- log_file_claim red on the staged set, naming the one path the recovered log cited that nobody had written. A guard that reads a lap's own citations against the tree is why the shelf came across instead of the claim going quietly false.
obs A second red arrived the same way, and it was the parked lap's own: rish_report_bound read sites=39 against a ceiling of 38, and `--sites` named the new one in one line. The witness said `say "${scan.out}"`, which opens a 4096-byte StrBuf and would end a run on a GREEN reading once this census outgrew it. Repaired to a bare `say scan.out`, the idiom reds_spine_derive already uses; sites back to 38.
obs Both of those reds were found by gates whose refusal NAMED its location -- one printed the path, one had a `--sites` flag its own detail line pointed at. That is the whole argument for the loom this lap measured and did not build.
obs Measured while the pass ran, and NOT repaired this lap: 23 fixture scans carry a `list` mode that names each offending item, and 22 of their witnesses call the scan in `count` mode. Twenty-one carry the route to those names only in a HEADER COMMENT, invisible to a reader watching 173 guards go by. stash_record is one of the two exceptions, and it is the only reason this lap found its red in one line rather than by hand.
obs That is %592's shape firing a second time, which by the reds-first law is a lantern becoming a loom. The repair shape is already proven in this tree -- fleet_round_open prints `-- sh $SCAN list` beside a nonzero count -- so the loom wants the shape held rather than invented.
